[Music]  now the former  south african president jacob zuma has  given five days to hand himself in  after judge sentenced him to 15 months  in jail  zuma's been found guilty of contempt for  failing to appear before an inquiry  investigating corruption  during his time in office zain basravi  has its report  for the first time a former south  african leader  is facing jail time jacob zuma has been  ordered to surrender himself to police  within five days  he was sentenced to 15 months for  failing to appear at a corruption  hearing  the constitutional court judge's  decision leaves the former president  with few legal options  and the extent to which mr zuma has  attempted to justify his defiance  through public statements is of no  relevance  the statements were not formally pleaded  so fault to be disregarded  the constitutional court can do nothing  but  conclude that mr zuma is guilty of  the crime of content of court  zuma resigned from office in 2018 after  his own party threatened  to pass a vote of no confidence against  him over allegations  of high-level financial corruption and  graft during his time as president  and deputy president he's always  maintained his innocence  but has not cooperated with  investigators may i ask you then  as to how you plead to the charges that  have been put to you  i plead not guilty allegations against  zuma include many titans of industry  whom he is said to have allowed to  plunder state resources  and influence state policy south  africa's chief justice has described  zuma's defense  and his attempt to garner public support  as insulting to the people of the  country  years of corruption scandals have left  south africa's new leaders  with an unenviable task of restoring  investor confidence  in africa's most industrialized economy  al jazeera let's speak to our  correspondent familia miller who is in  johannesburg the judge there pretty  damning in her ruling how is this  verdict gone down in south africa  well i think there's a couple of  different reactions  to this judgment by the constitutional  court  certainly observers commentators  academics  lording the court for the supremacy of  the rule of law  in that the constitutional court was  unambiguous when it said that  jacob zuma his actions have defied  the courts defied the commission of  inquiry  into corruption in south africa by him  not  wanting to testify at that inquiry  despite a number of appeals uh  including a court summons and so in  terms of of the supremacy of the  constitutional court south africa's  constitution  and that no one is above the law this is  very important  especially in the context of trying to  fight  large-scale corruption in this country  but jacob zuma also has his supporters  in the months that have preceded this  judgment he has said that he's been  vilified  that there's a political agenda against  him um  and that he's been treated unfairly and  that the constitutional court and other  courts are being used  to target the former president and there  are people who believe that  that this is part of a political agenda  in terms of the difficulties  the african national congress is facing  with regard to its fight to corruption  that party is divided and if people  were to be targeted or had to be charged  for being involved in corruption  there are a number of of high-ranking  anc individuals or leadership that could  potentially face the music and so this  is concerning for people who say  that the anc is being targeted the  former president's being targeted  and this isn't really about corruption  but really about  politics and what's going on in the  country at the moment so  there has been a mixed reaction to this  verdict to this judgment  and i suppose many are waiting to see  what happens within the next five days  when it's expected that jacob zuma  should hand himself over to police many  thanks for that for me to miller there  for us in  johannesburg we can get a little more  analysis on this joining us on skype  from cape town is sanusha naidu senior  research associate  at the institute for global dialogue  many thanks for joining us on the  newshour  look they could have fined zuma why do  you think the verdict was so strict  uh good afternoon i think the verdict  was strict particularly because  as your correspondent had indicated mr  zuma has constantly  tried many ways in which to manipulate  his  judicial process so he's constantly made  the assumption and  and made the accusation that he is part  of  a political campaign to discredit him to  target  him to malign him and so as much as he  theoretically says  that i want to have my day in court and  i'm willing to go to prison  if that if that is what will happen  he's not really giving any kind of  genuine commitment to those statements  he makes  he constantly argues that the judiciary  is biased against him he challenges the  legitimacy of the judiciary  and he challenges the credibility of the  judiciary he has also challenged  the the the constitutional court  justices as well as the various judges  in uh our judicial process he's also  went out and said that  they are targeting him and they have a  political agenda against him this has  caused a lot of tension on the ground  where the supporters of mr zuma have  come out  and made similar statements and also  gone to the point of threatening  uh the the the lives of judges and and  and basically putting those threats on  social media spaces so i think this  judgment is critical  to say to the to to the broader south  african public  that nobody even if it's mr zuma a  former head of state  he's not above the law but i think what  makes mr zuma's case even more important  and strategic  is that in that judgment read by the  constitutional justice campaign  she mentioned that you know mr zuma is  exceptional but not exceptional where  he's above the law he's exceptional  because  he was a former state president and he  knows  what the constitution says and he  actually took office  and he abided by the oath of that  presidency that he took so he is not  above the law in that context  sunush it's interesting uh just reading  through some comments from people on  twitter  uh very skeptical that he's actually  going to end up in jail  what do you think is going to happen is  he going to uphold or dear to this  mandate does he have a choice  um technically he doesn't have a choice  because this is the highest  apex court in the land this is the  constitutional court there's no higher  court than this there's no process  for his legal team to appeal this  decision there's nowhere to go  so technically speaking if he doesn't  abide by the ruling  and and and what the justices had ruled  in terms of giving him five days before  he then presents himself to a police  station uh  and and says i'm ready to be to serve  this 15-month sentence  then of course he is in he is in a  bigger set of  of legal uh dilemma and troubles but i  think what  what people are possibly feeling on the  ground and within the court of public  opinion  is that we've seen this before where mr  zuma has come to this tipping point  and of course something happens and then  he's pulled back or something happens  and  he justice doesn't prevail but i think  this time around  it is going to prevail i think that at  the end of the day where i'm very  concerned  is what happens in uh the support base  of mr jacob zuma  especially those those sections of the  population and  in the party that support him and have  been guarding his house the mk  soldiers they incontinent soldiers of  the african national congress which is  not a legitimate  kind of uh association but they've been  standing guard outside his homestead in  kwazulu-nutel  in kanjur and they've been arguing as  well that they're not going to let  anybody put their hands on mr jacob zuma  so i think that for me is where  a lot of the challenges lie in terms of  the  powder keg and the explosiveness of what  transpires here after  and whether mr zuma uh does actually  present himself to the  to the police station he having said  that i think his family have indicated  in tweets  that they will actually take him to the  to to the core to the to the  police station because their argument is  that mr mr zuma is fearless he's not  afraid to go to  to prison and more importantly they want  him to serve this term  in kwazulu-natal his home province and  not in khao teng  so i think at the end of the day uh  there's indications that  you know he's going to go but i think  you know at the end on the other side of  the spectrum  is what the supporters do we'll have to  see he's gone five days sanusha naido  good to talk to you senior research  associate at the institute for global  dialogue

